<section style="padding:0 24px;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto 56px;">
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 18px;">What an Industrial Generator Maintenance Contract Covers</h2>
    <div class="rich"><p>A maintenance contract should state intervals, tasks, parts and response obligations in writing, not in general terms. Ours is built on running hours with a calendar backstop, because a bank branch set at 400 hours a year and a factory set at 4,000 hours a year cannot share one schedule.</p><ul><li><strong>250 running hours or 3 months:</strong> lube oil and filter change, fuel filter renewal, air filter check, belt tension, battery and charger condition, leak inspection and an on load run.</li><li><strong>500 running hours:</strong> the above plus coolant chemistry and top up, air filter renewal, hose and clamp inspection, alternator bearing check and control panel function check.</li><li><strong>1,000 to 1,500 running hours:</strong> valve lash adjustment, injector spray pattern test, turbocharger shaft play check, spectrographic oil analysis and a thermographic scan of alternator terminals, breaker and panel busbars.</li><li><strong>6,000 to 8,000 running hours:</strong> top end overhaul window for most medium speed industrial sets, confirmed by compression and blow by readings.</li><li><strong>12,000 to 18,000 running hours:</strong> major overhaul window, subject to oil analysis trend, oil consumption rate and crankcase pressure.</li></ul><p>Intervals vary by manufacturer and by duty. Where OEM documentation is available we follow it and state so in the contract; where a set has been rebuilt or the plate data is gone, we set intervals from condition data and revise them after two service cycles.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section style="padding:0 24px;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto 56px;">
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 18px;">Failure Modes We See on Nigerian Industrial Gensets</h2>
    <div class="rich"><p>Most emergency call outs trace back to a small number of repeat causes. Nearly all of them are visible months in advance to anyone reading the service data, which is the argument for a contract rather than a repair budget.</p><ul><li><strong>Wet stacking from prolonged light load.</strong> Diesel sets held below roughly 30 percent of rating glaze the bores, foul injectors and pass unburnt fuel into the exhaust, which shows as oil at the stack, black smoke and lost output.</li><li><strong>Fuel contamination.</strong> Water ingress, sediment and microbial growth in bulk tanks block filters, erode injectors and stall sets under load. Tank cleaning and a polishing regime cost a fraction of an injection pump.</li><li><strong>Cooling system neglect.</strong> Blocked radiator cores, weak or plain water coolant, failed thermostats and slipping belts raise cylinder head temperature and produce cracked heads and gasket failure.</li><li><strong>AVR and excitation faults.</strong> Voltage hunting, over voltage trips and loss of residual magnetism are usually a failed AVR, a broken sensing lead or a damaged exciter rectifier, not a dead alternator.</li><li><strong>Starting system neglect.</strong> Sulphated batteries and failed chargers are the most common reason a mechanically healthy set does not answer a mains outage.</li><li><strong>Deferred top end work.</strong> Rising oil consumption, crankcase pressure and falling compression are ignored until a valve drops or a liner scores, turning a top end job into a major overhaul.</li></ul><p>We trend these through oil analysis, compression testing, thermography and load test records so the deterioration is priced into a planned outage instead of arriving as a shutdown. Where the driven plant is also in question, our <a href="/rotating-equipment-services-nigeria/">rotating equipment services</a> cover the pumps, gearboxes and compressors around the set.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section style="padding:0 24px;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto 56px;">
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 18px;">Load Bank Testing, Black Start and Standby Assurance</h2>
    <div class="rich"><p>Sites carrying critical load, hospitals, data rooms, bank operations centres and telecom facilities, need proof the set will accept load, not just proof it will start. A monthly no load exercise proves very little and actively damages a diesel engine over time.</p><p>A full resistive load bank test runs the set through 25, 50, 75 and 100 percent of rated output, holding at least one hour at full load, with readings logged every 15 minutes: voltage and frequency at each step, exhaust gas temperature per bank, coolant and lube oil temperature, alternator winding temperature and fuel consumption. Where transient performance matters we also test block load acceptance and voltage and frequency recovery time against the performance classes in ISO 8528-5. The output is a signed test record stating the capacity the set actually delivered, any derating found and the corrective work required.</p><p>Black start assurance goes further. For plants that must start on genset before restoring the wider system, we verify battery bank capacity under cranking load, air start pressure and receiver capacity, day tank and priming arrangements, ATS transfer time and first attempt start success over repeated cold starts. On sites where a genset backs a turbine installation, the same records feed the outage plan for <a href="/steam-turbine-overhaul-nigeria/">steam and gas turbine overhaul</a>.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section style="padding:0 24px;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto 56px;">
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 18px;">Scope, Lead Times and What Goes Into the Purchase Order</h2>
    <div class="rich"><p>Procurement cannot raise an order against a verbal estimate. Our proposals carry line item scope, part numbers where the OEM is known, labour hours, mobilisation, outage window and payment milestones, priced in Naira with a USD equivalent shown for comparison.</p><ul><li><strong>Preventive maintenance contract, 250 to 500 kVA:</strong> NGN 1,800,000 to NGN 3,500,000 per unit per year (about USD 1,150 to USD 2,250) for four scheduled visits with oil, filters and consumables included.</li><li><strong>Top end overhaul, 500 kVA class:</strong> NGN 9,000,000 to NGN 18,000,000 (about USD 5,800 to USD 11,600), 5 to 8 working days on site once parts are in hand.</li><li><strong>Major overhaul, 1 MVA class:</strong> NGN 35,000,000 to NGN 70,000,000 (about USD 22,500 to USD 45,000), 3 to 5 weeks on site depending on findings.</li><li><strong>Resistive load bank test, up to 1,000 kVA:</strong> NGN 850,000 to NGN 1,600,000 (about USD 550 to USD 1,000) per set per test, report included.</li><li><strong>AVR replacement and commissioning:</strong> NGN 450,000 to NGN 2,200,000 (about USD 290 to USD 1,400) depending on alternator make and controller integration.</li></ul><p>Ranges assume a rate near NGN 1,550 to the dollar and move with it. Imported OEM parts typically run 4 to 8 weeks from order to Lagos before Form M, PAAR and port clearance are counted, so we confirm the parts list from inspection first and book the outage window only when critical items are in country. A fuller breakdown sits on our <a href="/generator-turbine-maintenance-cost/">generator and turbine maintenance cost</a> page.</p><p>Mobilisation is quoted from Lagos as a separate line item, with routes and spares logistics into <a href="/turbine-services-lagos/">Lagos</a>, <a href="/turbine-services-port-harcourt/">Port Harcourt</a>, <a href="/turbine-services-abuja/">Abuja</a>, <a href="/turbine-services-kaduna/">Kaduna</a>, <a href="/turbine-services-warri/">Warri</a> and <a href="/turbine-services-kano/">Kano</a>. To begin, book a plant assessment or request a technical proposal on info@axiompower.com.ng or +234 803 000 0000.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section style="padding:72px 24px;background:#EAF5FB;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto;">
    <span class="kicker">FAQ</span>
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(24px,3.5vw,36px);margin:0 0 32px;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px;">
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #e3ded4;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Nunito Sans',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#0A3D5C;margin:0 0 10px;">How often should an industrial generator be serviced in Nigeria?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">Service on running hours with a calendar backstop: a minor service at 250 hours or every 3 months, a fuller service at 500 hours, and a major inspection with oil analysis at 1,000 to 1,500 hours. A factory set running 8 hours a day reaches 250 hours in about five weeks, so the hour meter and not the calendar must drive the schedule. Dusty inland sites and coastal locations normally need air and fuel filter intervals shortened by about a third.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #e3ded4;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Nunito Sans',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#0A3D5C;margin:0 0 10px;">What does a generator maintenance contract cost in Nigeria?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">For a 250 to 500 kVA industrial set, expect NGN 1,800,000 to NGN 3,500,000 per unit per year (about USD 1,150 to USD 2,250) covering four scheduled visits with oil, filters and consumables. Sets above 1 MVA and multi unit sites are priced per unit against a site rate, and mobilisation outside Lagos is a separate line. We issue a fixed price contract after a plant assessment rather than a rate card, because run hours and set condition move the figure more than kVA does.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #e3ded4;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Nunito Sans',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#0A3D5C;margin:0 0 10px;">How long does a generator overhaul take and how much downtime should we plan?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">A top end overhaul on a 500 kVA class set takes 5 to 8 working days on site, and a major overhaul on a 1 MVA set takes 3 to 5 weeks. The larger variable is parts: imported OEM components typically take 4 to 8 weeks to land before clearance, so we confirm the parts list from inspection and only book the outage window when the critical items are in country. Sites with a second set or a rental bridge normally keep production running throughout.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #e3ded4;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Nunito Sans',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#0A3D5C;margin:0 0 10px;">Why does our standby generator smoke and pass oil into the exhaust?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">That is wet stacking, caused by running a diesel engine below roughly 30 percent of rated load for extended periods. Unburnt fuel and soot glaze the cylinder bores and collect in the exhaust, which cuts output, fouls injectors and forces an early top end overhaul. The correction is a resistive load bank exercise at 75 to 100 percent load for at least one hour, followed by either a permanent change in load allocation or right sizing the set.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #e3ded4;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Nunito Sans',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#0A3D5C;margin:0 0 10px;">Do you cover sites outside Lagos, and how is that handled?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">Yes. We mobilise from Lagos to Rivers, FCT, Kaduna, Delta and Kano States, covering Port Harcourt, Warri, Abuja, Kaduna and Kano. Mobilisation, travel and per diem are quoted as separate line items so procurement can see them before the order is raised. For contract sites outside Lagos we batch scheduled visits across units and hold an agreed critical spares list at site to keep response times inside the contract.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
